    subsumised as RNA damages, can irreversibly disrupt their
    normal structure, stability, or function and thus have severe
    consequences for cellular function and overall organismal
    health. For example, RNA exposed to reactive oxygen
    species (ROS), alkylating agents, or other attacks displays
    bases and sugar-phosphate backbone alterations, perturbed
    RNA stability and functionality in protein biosynthesis.[2,3]
    Physical factors such as UV radiation or ionizing radiation
    can cause breaks in RNA strands or cross-linking of RNA
    molecules, leading to structural changes or fragmentation.[4,5]
    Besides, some viruses target host RNA for degradation or
    modification to evade host immune responses or to hijack
    cellular machinery for viral replication.[6–8]
    Since RNA exhibits such a variety of damage types, it is
    highly plausible that cells should have developed a corresponding repair mechanism, similar to DNA repair and
    protein repair (Figure 1A).[9–11] Nevertheless, research on
    RNA repair mechanisms has lagged behind that of DNA
    and proteins.[12–18] The discovery of hABH3 in 2003 marked
    a key breakthrough, as it was shown to demethylate
    alkylated RNA damages like N1-methyladenosine (m1A)
    and 3-methylcytidine (m3C).[14] However, hABH3 also
    targets DNA lesions with similar efficiency, indicating a lack
