    Spotlight
    6
    
    Poly(m A) tails stabilize transcripts
    Guillaume Lavergne1 and Jean-Yves Roignant1,2,*
    1Center for Integrative Genomics, Génopode Building, Faculty of Biology and Medicine, University of Lausanne, CH-1015, Lausanne,
    Switzerland
    2Institute of Pharmaceutical and Biomedical Sciences, Johannes Gutenberg-University Mainz, Staudingerweg 5, 55128 Mainz, Germany
    *Correspondence: jean-yves.roignant@unil.ch
    https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molcel.2022.05.017
    
    Viegas et al. (2022) discover that in Trypanosoma brucei the poly(A) tails of the variant surface glycoprotein
    (VSG) transcripts are methylated, a mechanism that stabilizes these transcripts and ensures protection
    against the immune response in mammals.
    Trypanosoma brucei (T. brucei) are unicellular parasites that cause sleeping sickness in humans, a fatal tropical disease,
    and nagana in cattle (Matthews, 2005).
    They are found in the bloodstream of their
    mammalian hosts and are transmitted by
    the tsetse fly, where they proliferate in
    the gut and differentiate in the salivary
    glands in preparation for new infections.
    In order to adapt to these different
    environments, trypanosomes undergo
    complex and coordinated programmed
    development. For instance, in their bloodstream form (BSF), trypanosomes can
    evade antibody responses through the
    sequential expression of antigenically
    distinct variable surface glycoproteins
    (VSGs) (Mugnier et al., 2015). These VSG
    transcripts are the most abundant in
    BSF and their accumulation partially depends on their unusually long half-life.
    Upon uptake by the tsetse, VSG transcripts are rapidly degraded and the
    VSG coat is replaced by a less-dense surface coat composed of procyclins (procyclic form, PCF). While these cellular
    changes have been well described, the
    mechanism underlying VSG transcript
    stability as well as their decay upon differentiation to PCF has remained unclear. In
    a recent study published in Nature, Viegas
    et al. show that a conserved element in
    the 30 UTR of VSG transcripts is required
    for catalyzing the N6-methyladenosine
    (m6A) RNA modification in their poly(A)
    tails, which in turn prolongs their half-life
    by impeding deadenylation (Viegas
    et al., 2022).
    m6A is the most abundant internal
    mRNA modification in eukaryotes and is
    a major regulator of mRNA metabolism
    (Shi et al., 2019; Zaccara et al., 2019). In
    
    mammals, the mark is enriched at the
    beginning of the last exon and deposited
    by a large methyltransferase complex
    comprising the catalytic component
    METTL3. In order to address whether
    RNA modifications contribute to gene
    regulation in T. brucei, the authors used
    liquid chromatography-tandem mass
    spectrometry to measure the levels of
    multiple marks. They found that only
    m6A is enriched in mRNA relative to total
    RNA and is found equally in BSF and
    PCF. They next performed m6A RNA
    immunoprecipitation using BSF wholetranscriptome extracts and found that
    VSG transcripts are the most methylated.
    The methylation level of these transcripts
    is estimated to represent 50% of the
    entire m6A signal from total RNA by immunoblotting. Surprisingly, this signal arises
    entirely from the poly(A) tails of the VSG
    transcripts. These data indicate that the
    methylation profile in T. brucei is radically
    different from other eukaryotes and suggest a novel function for the m6A
    modification.
    To address the role of this methylation,
    the authors examine the decay kinetics of
    VSG transcripts after either inhibition of
    transcription in BSF, or induction of differentiation to PCF, in relation to the level of
    m6A and the size of poly(A) tails. Notably,
    they could show that in both cases the
    loss of m6A precedes the shortening of
    poly(A) tails and the subsequent transcript
    degradation. This suggested that m6A in
    poly(A) tails might stabilize VSG transcripts, and its removal is a prerequisite
    for mRNA decay. Testing this hypothesis
    by simply removing m6A is not straightforward as the machinery required to install
    m6A on poly(A) tails is not known—and
    
    is clearly different from the canonical
    deposition in mammals, as METTL3 is
    not conserved in trypanosomes. Nevertheless RNA-FISH analysis and m6A immunostaining suggested that the methylation occurs in the nucleus as has been
    reported in other organisms.
    Previous work indicated that a
    conserved 16-mer motif in the 30 UTR of
    VSG transcripts is necessary for their stability (Ridewood et al., 2017). This raised
    the possibility that this motif is in some
    way connected to m6A inclusion in the
    poly(A) tails. Indeed, m6A quantification
    and mutagenesis analysis revealed that
    the presence of m6A in the poly(A)
    tails strongly correlates with an intact
    motif. Accordingly, blocking transcription
    shows premature shortening of poly(A)
    tails and rapid decay of transcripts containing a mutated motif. Finally, the
    authors demonstrate that the decay of
    VSG transcripts mainly occurs in the cytoplasm through the activity of CAF1, the
    catalytic component of the deadenylase
    complex. Altogether, these data are
    consistent with a model in which m6A in
    the poly(A) tails of VSG mRNA stabilizes
    these transcripts, allowing high accumulation of VSG proteins and ensuring protection against the activation of the
    mammalian immune response. Upon uptake by the tsetse, the removal of m6A
    from the poly(A) tails leads to rapid decay
    and the replacement of the VSG coat with
    procyclins, which are better suited for survival in the fly environment (Figure 1).
    m6A has been shown to play various
    roles in mammals, including mRNA decay
    through YTHDF-mediated deadenylation
    (Zaccara and Jaffrey, 2020). Other m6A
    readers, such as members of the IGF2BP
    
    Molecular Cell 82, June 2, 2022 ª 2022 Elsevier Inc. 1979
    
