    ABSTRACT: The chemical biology of native nucleic acid modifications has seen an intense upswing, first
    concerning DNA modifications in the field of epigenetics and then concerning RNA modifications in a field
    that was correspondingly rebaptized epitranscriptomics by analogy. The German Research Foundation
    (DFG) has funded several consortia with a scientific focus in these fields, strengthening the traditionally
    well-developed nucleic acid chemistry community and inciting it to team up with colleagues from the life
    sciences and data science to tackle interdisciplinary challenges. This Perspective focuses on the genesis,
    scientific outcome, and downstream impact of the DFG priority program SPP1784 and offers insight into how it fecundated further
    consortia in the field. Pertinent research was funded from mid-2015 to 2022, including an extension related to the coronavirus
    pandemic. Despite being a detriment to research activity in general, the pandemic has resulted in tremendously boosted interest in
    the field of RNA and RNA modifications as a consequence of their widespread and successful use in vaccination campaigns against
    SARS-CoV-2. Funded principal investigators published over 250 pertinent papers with a very substantial impact on the field. The
    program also helped to redirect numerous laboratories toward this dynamic field. Finally, SPP1784 spawned initiatives for several
    funded consortia that continue to drive the fields of nucleic acid modification.
    
    ■
    
    PREAMBLE: THE FIELD AT THE TIME OF
    APPLICATION IN 2013
    
    technique of immunoprecipitation had already been known
    since the 1980s. The conjunction with high-throughput
    sequencing revived a sense of importance of mRNA
    modifications for the regulation of gene expression in the
    international community. In this context, the term “epitranscriptomics” was coined shortly thereafter, implying that RNA
    modifications contain information that goes beyond their
    nucleotide sequence context. Although clearly conceived under
    the impression of mRNA modification, modifications of
    noncoding RNAs, including tRNA, rRNA, and others, are
    also subsumed in the epitranscriptome.
    Under the impression of highly dynamic developments in
    the two closely interrelated fields outlined above, the
    application for the establishment of the priority program
    “Chemical Biology of Native Nucleic Acid Modifications”
    emerged, which was submitted in October 2012 and approved
    as SPP1784 in April 2013.
    
    In the field of epigenetics, the structures of 5-substituted
    cytosines, which arise from the successive oxidation of the
    modified nucleobase 5mC (5-methylcytosine) in DNA, had
    become well-known in the years around 2009. Their discovery,
    mapping, and functional characterization as a new level of
    information transfer beyond the DNA sequence prompted
    intense research activity on DNA modifications, in part
    because of their apparent direct consequences for the
    regulation of gene expression.1
    At that time, the field of RNA modifications was
    characterized by steady but slow progress on noncoding
    RNAs of the translation apparatus, i.e., tRNAs and rRNA.2−4
    The overwhelming majority of papers focused on unicellular
    organisms, and only a fraction of those working on eukaryotes
    looked into mRNA modifications.5,6
    Possibly as a consequence of technical developments in the
    DNA field, mRNA modifications became the focus of the
    community, especially when in 2012 two widely noticed
    publications7,8 analyzed the distribution of m6A (N6-methyladenosine) nucleosides after MeRIP (Methylated RNA
    Immunoprecipitation) and by high-throughput sequencing.
    Interestingly, both the presence of m6A in mRNA and the
