    Input header text:
    Articles
    https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-021-01250-z
    
    Nascent Ribo-Seq measures ribosomal loading time
    and reveals kinetic impact on ribosome density
    Johanna Schott
    Anjana Shenoy
    
    ✉, Sonja Reitter1,2, Doris Lindner1,2, Jan Grosser
    
    , Marius Bruer 1,2,
    3
    , Tamar Geiger3, Arthur Mathes4,5, Gergana Dobreva4,5 and Georg Stoecklin
    1,2
    
    1,2
    
    1,2
    
    In general, mRNAs are assumed to be loaded with ribosomes instantly upon entry into the cytoplasm. To measure ribosome
    density (RD) on nascent mRNA, we developed nascent Ribo-Seq by combining Ribo-Seq with progressive 4-thiouridine labeling. In mouse macrophages, we determined experimentally the lag between the appearance of nascent mRNA and its association with ribosomes, which was calculated to be 20–22 min for bulk mRNA. In mouse embryonic stem cells, nRibo-Seq revealed
    an even stronger lag of 35–38 min in ribosome loading. After stimulation of macrophages with lipopolysaccharide, the lag
    between cytoplasmic and translated mRNA leads to uncoupling between input and ribosome-protected fragments, which gives
    rise to distorted RD measurements under conditions where mRNA amounts are far from steady-state expression. As a result,
    we demonstrate that transcriptional changes affect RD in a passive way.
    
    R
    
    apid changes in gene expression occur in almost all biological systems, for example, when cells respond to specific
    ligands or alteration of environmental conditions. Typically,
    transcriptional changes go hand in hand with regulation of mRNA
    export and stability, translation efficiency and protein stability, all
    of which determine the final protein output. Therefore, it can be
    difficult to disentangle the contribution of each individual process,
    especially when gene expression is not in steady-state conditions.
    Translational control of specific mRNAs plays a key role in regulating gene expression, and numerous mechanisms that regulate
    translation have been identified1. Methods developed to measure
    translation efficiency have to take changes in mRNA abundance
    into account. For instance, when the association of mRNAs with
    ribosomes is measured by fractionation of sucrose gradients, the
    amount of polysome-bound mRNA is divided by the amount of free
    mRNA2. Ribosome profiling (Ribo-Seq) also quantifies RD by comparing ribosome-protected fragments with randomly fragmented
    cytoplasmic mRNA3. Likewise, the assessment of protein production by nascent proteomics allows calculation of translation rates
    when related to mRNA abundance4. A frequent assumption for such
    measurements is that changes in cytoplasmic mRNA abundance are
    instantly reflected by corresponding changes in polysomal mRNA2.
    However, if newly transcribed mRNA needs time to be loaded with
    ribosomes, changes in polysomal mRNA would lag behind changes
    in cytoplasmic mRNA, and influence translation analysis5. In this
    study, we developed nascent Ribo-Seq (nRibo-Seq) to measure the
    lag caused by ribosome loading, and explored its impact on the
    determination of translation efficiencies.
    
    Results
    
    Establishment of nRibo-Seq in macrophages. To measure the
    time required for ribosome loading, we developed nRibo-Seq (Fig.
    1a), which is based on progressive metabolic labeling of RNA using
    4-thiouridine (4sU), followed by assessment of RD on nascent
    
    mRNA by Ribo-Seq. We chose a concentration of 200 µM 4sU and
    a maximum incubation time of 2 h, where global translation is not
    affected (Supplementary Fig. 1). As illustrated in Fig. 1a, cytoplasmic RNA of 4sU-labeled cells was either fragmented randomly by
    alkaline hydrolysis (input (IN)) or purified from monosomes after
    subjecting the lysate to a limited digest with RNaseI (footprint
    (FP); Supplementary Fig. 2). Subsequently, both IN and FP RNA
    was treated with sodium periodate in a buffer containing ammonium chloride, which converts 4sU (1) to cytidine ((C) 3) via a
    2-oxopyrimidine-4-sulfonate intermediate6 (2; Fig. 1b). The efficiency of 4sU-to-C conversion was monitored by Sanger sequencing of a synthetic oligonucleotide containing 4sU at a single position
    (Fig. 1c), and the same oligonucleotide was added to each nRibo-Seq
    sample as a spike-in to assess sample-specific conversion efficiency
    after Illumina sequencing (Extended Data Fig. 1a,b). With a conversion rate of ~94%, this reaction is highly efficient. A similar, albeit
    chemically different, approach was applied by Herzog et al. for the
    SLAMseq method7, and by Schofield et al. for the TimeLapse-seq
    method8. It should be noted that our method applies the conversion reaction to RNA fragments and not to full-length RNA, where
    structured regions reduce the efficiency of 4sU-to-C conversion9.
    After Illumina sequencing, the reads containing T-to-C transitions were identified bioinformatically. In mouse RAW264.7 macrophages, the 4sU incorporation rate increases over time from 2%
    to 14% (Extended Data Fig. 1c,d), presumably because of gradual
    uptake and accumulation of 4sU by the cells. This results in a gradient of T-to-C density along transcripts (Supplementary Fig. 3) and
    demonstrates that our protocol removes nuclear material efficiently.
    Because Ribo-Seq produces short fragments of only 29–30 nt in
    length, most reads derived from nascent RNA do not cover any 4sU
    incorporation sites (Fig. 1d and Extended Data Fig. 2). Therefore,
    it is necessary to estimate the number of nascent fragments lacking T-to-C transitions on the basis of the binomial distribution.
    An accurate estimation was not possible at the level of individual
    
